<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iga Ueno Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ueno Castle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iga Ueno Castle is a castle in the city of Iga in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mie-prefecture">Mie Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. First built in the 1580s, it was rebuilt from 1611 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todo-takatora">Tōdō Takatora</a>, lord of the Tsu domain, who gave it stone walls about 30 metres high, among the tallest in Japan. Its keep was blown down in a storm in 1612 before completion and never rebuilt; the present wooden keep dates from 1935.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iga-ueno-castle.jpg" alt="Iga Ueno Castle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was T@ka at Japanese Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igauenozyo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The castle stands on a hill at the north-western corner of a plateau formed by the Kizu and Tsuge rivers, in the centre of Iga. The city lies in a mountain basin on an important route linking the old capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ise-grand-shrine">Ise Grand Shrine</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Iga Province, ringed by mountains, was governed from around the 1460s by its warrior families, later known as ninja, as the Iga ikki, with a headquarters on the site of the present castle, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> conquered it in 1581.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Construction began in 1585 on the orders of Takigawa Katsutoshi. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, Tsutsui Sadatsugu was moved from Kōriyama Castle to rule Iga; he divided the hill into an upper eastern and a lower western half, with the innermost bailey, or honmaru, on the upper part and a three-storey keep on its eastern edge.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Battle of Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> expelled Tsutsui for siding with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> and gave Iga and northern Ise to his trusted general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todo-takatora">Tōdō Takatora</a>, already the builder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uwajima">Uwajima</a> and Imabari castles. Tōdō moved the inner bailey to the western half of the hill and added terraces over 100 metres long to east and west, giving the castle an overall extent of 800 by 400 metres. His new five-storey keep was still being built when the storm destroyed it, and once the Toyotomi were destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-osaka">Siege of Osaka</a> in 1615 the castle lost its importance, so the keep and the planned moats and walls were never finished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>All the remaining buildings were destroyed after the Meiji restoration. The wooden keep of 1935 houses a museum of the area's history, while the rest of the castle lies in ruins within Ueno Park, its high honmaru walls still standing. It has been a National Historic Site since 1967, and the Iga-ryū Ninja Museum stands beside it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iga Ueno Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
